---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CaseStudyHeader from '../../components/notebook/CaseStudyHeader.astro';
import CaseHero from '../../components/notebook/CaseHero.astro';
import SectionRow from '../../components/notebook/SectionRow.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/notebook/Figure.astro';

import hackathon from '../../assets/echos/hackathon-prototype.png';
import researchIdeation from '../../assets/echos/research-ideation.png';
import researchClusters from '../../assets/echos/research-clusters.png';
---

<Base title="echos · VK">
  <div style="background: var(--paper); min-height: 100vh; color: var(--ink); padding-bottom: 120px">

    <CaseStudyHeader entryNum="" />

    <CaseHero
      eyebrow="echos · XHacks → independent"
      title="Leave a voice note pinned to a place"
      lede="A voice-based, location-based social app: leave short voice notes pinned to places for friends, strangers, or your future self to find. Started at XHacks, now being redesigned solo."
      meta={[
        { label: "Status", value: "In progress — hi-fi prototype unfinished" },
        { label: "Origin", value: "XHacks hackathon → independent redesign" },
        { label: "Role", value: "Design, research" },
        { label: "Tools", value: "Figma" },
      ]}
      note="the idea: a city full of voice notes only you can hear, left by people who were here before you.<br><br>the problem: when should it play, and when should it stay quiet?"
    />

    <SectionRow num="01" label="the idea">
      <Fragment slot="main">
        <p class="body">
          Digital memories don't attach to places. You can photograph a spot, geotag it, pin it on a
          map, but the experience of being there doesn't carry through. echos is an attempt at
          something different: short voice notes left at a location, findable by anyone who comes
          close enough to hear them.
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          The medium is voice specifically because it carries what a photo doesn't: hesitation,
          excitement, an accent, a laugh. And because it lowers the barrier — no polishing, no
          composition, no posting anxiety. You speak, and someone nearby finds it later.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="margin">
        <p class="note" style="margin:0;transform:rotate(-1deg)">
          the trail feature is based on real places. "Italy": Sorrento, Cortona, a gelato place.
          the capsule feature is based on real events. a birthday. it works best when it's true.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
    </SectionRow>

    <SectionRow num="02" label="the research">
      <Fragment slot="main">
        <p class="body">
          Research covered three age groups — adolescents, young adults, adults — looking at how
          each shares places and keeps memories. The common thread: no good way to leave something
          at a place that another person will find. Photos get buried in camera rolls. Text is too
          low-stakes for the moment. Voice fills the gap: intimate enough to feel personal,
          low-effort enough to actually happen.
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          The design values that came out of research: authenticity, the vulnerability of voice,
          discovery and serendipity, no pressure to perform for an audience. No feed, no likes,
          no follower count.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
    </SectionRow>

    <Figure
      fig="fig. 1"
      wide
      caption="Research clusters by age group (left) and pain points and solution values (right). The overlap across all three groups: no meaningful way to leave and revisit memories tied to a physical place."
      note="the values map (right) became the design guardrails. anything that added pressure or polish violated them."
      images={[
        { src: researchClusters, alt: "Research affinity clusters organized by three participant age groups: adolescents, young adults, adults." },
        { src: researchIdeation, alt: "Pain points and user needs on the left; solution values for echos on the right: personalization, authenticity, vulnerability of voice, commitment, discovery, no pressure." },
      ]}
    />

    <SectionRow num="03" label="where it is now">
      <Fragment slot="main">
        <p class="body">
          The XHacks prototype covers the core loop: map view, echos nearby, record and post, four
          listening modes (notify, playlist, proximity, proximity ensemble). The redesign adds
          structure: trails (a path of echos through a place), capsules (echos that open at a future
          date), and a friend layer. The hi-fi prototype is unfinished. This page will update when
          it is.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="margin">
        <p class="note" style="margin:0;transform:rotate(0.8deg)">
          the listening modes are the most interesting design problem: how much control should you
          have over what plays? proximity ensemble (echos overlapping by distance) is the most
          surprising, and probably the most annoying.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
    </SectionRow>

    <Figure
      fig="fig. 2"
      wide
      caption="XHacks hackathon prototype: splash, map view, echos nearby, record screen, and four listening modes. Warm peach palette, stacked 'echoes' logotype."
      note="the visual identity shifted in the redesign — softer purple, 'echos' (dropped the e). why: the name felt more like a thing than an action."
      images={[
        { src: hackathon, alt: "XHacks hackathon prototype overview showing 10 screens: splash screen with stacked 'echoes' type, map view with green pins, echoes list, record screen, and four listening mode screens (notify, playlist, proximity, proximity ensemble)." },
      ]}
    />

    <footer class="cs-row" style="padding-top: 24px; align-items: end">
      <div></div>
      <a href="/design" class="back mono">← Design</a>
      <div></div>
    </footer>

  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .body {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 21px;
    line-height: 1.62;
    margin: 0 0 22px;
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 20, 'wght' 400;
    max-width: none;
  }
  .body:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .back { color: var(--ink-60); text-decoration: none; font-size: 13px; }
  .back:hover { color: var(--ink); }
  @media (max-width: 1000px) { .body { font-size: 19px; } }
</style>
